#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""barry_launcher_shelld: Barry Launcher's apps and on-screen keyboard for
the AYN Thor's bottom screen.

Runs in barry_launcher_session next to the shell (shell/Shell.qml) and the
keyboard (shell/Keyboard.qml), which call it on 127.0.0.1:PORT:

  GET  /apps      {"browser": {"running": bool, "status": str, ...,
                  "frozen": bool}, ...}: status is what a tile says while an
                  app installs ("" otherwise); frozen while it waits in the
                  background (see below)
  POST /launch    {"app": name}: start the app, or bring the running one
                  forward
  POST /close     {"app": name}: quit it
  GET  /home      {"serial": n, "open": bool, "over": bool, "page": n,
                  "under": path, "app": bool, "lifted": [x, y] | null}:
                  Barry is open (its pages over
                  the hero art), over an app it closes back to, and serial
                  counts the times it came up, for its slide-in; page is the
                  one it opened on (0 the apps, 1 the dashboard, -1 the quick
                  settings: a swipe in from a side over an app), under a
                  picture of that app as it showed (a PNG path, or ""), for
                  the page to slide in over, and app whether an app (not a
                  tool) is in front with Barry closed: side swipes are on;
                  lifted where (screen pixels) the swipe up from the bottom
                  edge that opened it lifted, for no tap there just after
  POST /home      open Barry
  POST /barry     {"open": bool, "why": str, "page": n, "back": bool}: open
                  or close Barry (the shell: a tap on the game art, its
                  standby timeout, a side swipe over an app with its page);
                  closing goes back to the app Barry opened over only from a
                  side page (or with "back": true), else to the art
  POST /side-prepare {}: a finger came down at a side edge over an app:
                  take the app's picture now, for the side page it may pull
  POST /apps/install {"path": archive}: install or update a user app
                  (barry_apps), {"ok": bool, "app": manifest | "error": str}
  POST /apps/remove  {"app": id}: remove a user app and its data
  GET  /game-links   {"links": [{"game", "gameName", "app", "appName", "close"}]}
  POST /game-links   {"links": [...]}: the apps that open with games
  POST /game-event   {"game": appid, "running": bool}: a game started or
                  stopped (the Barry Launcher plugin, which sees Steam)
  GET  /keyboard  {"visible": bool, "top": bool}: top when the text field is
                  in the lower half, so the keyboard does not cover it
  POST /keyboard  {"visible": bool}: show or hide by hand
  GET  /keyd      what barry_launcher_keyd's rules need (keyd_state)
  POST /keyd      {"do": "show" | "hide" | "top" | "top-down", "why": "..."}: keyd's call
  GET  /top-keyboard {"serial": n, "visible": bool}: the Trackpad app's
                  keyboard, asked up (or down) each time serial counts on
  GET  /tools     {"serial": n, "leaving": {app: pid}}: the tools (the
                  trackpad, the Deck controls) asked to slide away before
                  Barry comes forward (tools_leave), by their process ids;
                  serial counts the asks
  POST /type      {"text": "...", "correct": word, ...}: autocorrect's plan
                  for Keyboard.qml, which types it ({"back": n, "text": s,
                  "key": k}: backspaces, then text, then a key)

A POST needs a JSON body, a Host of 127.0.0.1 or localhost and no Origin:
the apps here include a web browser, and a web page must not type.

The same state comes as events too, for the shell and the keyboard
(Barry.Input) instead of asking over and over: a Unix socket
(EVENTS_SOCKET, external-and-mods/barry-launcher-input/SHELLD-EVENTS.md)
sends each client every topic's GET answer as it connects (home, keyboard,
top-keyboard, keyd, apps, tools), then a line for a topic each time its
answer changes, one JSON object a line ({"topic": t, "data": answer}). A
client may send {"keyboard_height": n}, as GET /keyboard?h=n; a tool's
window {"tool": true} (it slides away when asked) and {"tool_left": true}
(it has). The answers are
kept ready (publish, at every change), and the GETs send those: nothing
here looks again unasked (apps ending: their pidfds; the user apps and the
home screen's order: file monitors).

When the keyboards come up is barry_launcher_keyd's (C++): it follows text
focus (AT-SPI; KWin's input method in Desktop Mode) and the touches, and
asks here (GET/POST /keyd) to show or hide them. This only says what it
needs to know: whether an app is opening (no keyboard then), the keyboard's
own hide, Barry open. The keyboard types into the focused window itself (Barry.Input,
XTest; KWin's input method in Desktop Mode); here it only asks how to
correct a word. The keyboard window is a gamescope overlay
(STEAM_OVERLAY), and while shown it has STEAM_INPUT_FOCUS=2, as Steam's own
keyboard does: it takes the touches, but X keyboard focus stays on the app
underneath. Like Steam's overlay it stays mapped and hides by opacity:
gamescope stops watching a window's properties once it is unmapped, so a
hidden overlay's marks could never be cleared. It also hides while Barry
is open, since overlays are drawn above it.

Most apps are Firefox instances, each with its own profile under
~/.local/share/barry_launcher: the browser, and Discord's web app in kiosk
mode (no address bar or tabs). Signal has no web app: it is
Signal Desktop from Flathub, installed for the user on its first start.
Trackpad drives the top screen instead (shell/TopInputApp.qml, with
Barry.Input's virtual mouse and keyboard), in a window of its own like the
rest: a trackpad
with a keyboard below it that slides away and back. A click on a top-screen
text field brings its keyboard up (barry_launcher_keyd); so does a launch of
"keyboard" (its Keyboard tile, and the Barry Launcher plugin in place of
Steam's keyboard).
Without Steam, gamescope treats every window
as its own app and shows the most recently mapped one (stacking order does
not count), so switching is unmapping and mapping the wanted window again.
Closing Barry back to an app is the other way round: Barry's own window is
unmapped, and the app, mapped under it all along, shows again as it was.
Mapping the app again repaints it from nothing: Signal showed a black or
half-drawn frame each time a side page closed.

Barry opens and closes over the home window, whose base is the selected
game's hero art (Shell.qml): BARRY_FLAG says whether it is open. A short
press of the AYN button flips it (sm8550-thor-backlightd), and this hears
the change (a file monitor) and brings the home window forward. Closing
(the AYN button, the shell's standby timeout) goes to the hero art; the
app Barry opened over stays in the background, a tile tap away. This
writes the flag too: an app started from Barry closes it, and a swipe up
from the bottom edge over an app opens it. A swipe in from the left edge
over an app opens it on the performance dashboard, from the right edge on
the quick settings, as a phone's side panels: Barry.Input's EdgeSwipe
follows the finger and the shell asks (POST /side-prepare, /barry with a
page); Shell.qml closes back to the app when they are swiped away (a
gesture on the app: the only close that goes back to it).

Apps in the background (Game Mode): each app (not a tool) runs in a
systemd user scope of its own (systemd-run --scope execs it in place, so
its pid and pidfd are the app's; Flatpak puts its apps in scopes itself),
and FREEZE_S after it left the screen (another app in front, Barry or the
art over it) its scope is frozen (cgroup v2, cgroup.freeze): no CPU until
it comes forward again, when it is thawed before its window shows. Not
while it plays sound (pactl subscribe: a stream of its own, not corked),
nor an app whose manifest says "keepAwake" (Signal). A frozen app is
closed BACKGROUND_CLOSE_S after it left the screen, and under memory
pressure (a PSI trigger) the frozen apps close, least recently used
first, one per trigger. Closing thaws first, so the app hears its SIGTERM.

User apps (barry_apps: QML apps installed from an archive, by the barry-app
command or the Barry Launcher plugin) join the home screen after the built-in
ones; each runs in shell/AppHost.qml, with its service if it has one
(started first, stopped when the app ends), their output in
~/.cache/barry_launcher/ID.log. They are picked up as they are installed.
Those that come with Barry Launcher (Dino) are installed at its start
unless the user removed them.
"""
